{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_013TzHm1onkQgNDWUjuV3ZKj", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_day_(computing)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/anniversary/birth-of-the-internet-a-historic-development-in-communications\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/anniversary/birth-of-the-internet-a-historic-development-in-communications/amp\"}, {\"url\": \"https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2009-November/000960.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://internetnews.com/news/30-years-of-tcp-ip-dominance-began-with-a-deadline\"}, {\"url\": \"https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2009-November/000961.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://telepolis.de/-3428133\"}, {\"url\": \"https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2023-August/008772.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://jisaku.com/glossary/tcp-ip-transition-1983\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET)))\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_day_(computing)))\"}, {\"url\": \"https://internetnews.com/news/30-years-of-tcp-ip-dominance-began-with-a-deadline))\"}, {\"url\": \"https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2023-August/008772.html))\"}, {\"url\": \"https://telepolis.de/-3428133))\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T13:15:54.895Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "ARPANET switch from NCP to TCP/IP January 1, 1983 flag day", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01RidoZdjpTkyUPDXMTKrvga", "content": [{"title": "Flag day (computing)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_day_(computing)"}, {"title": "Network Control Protocol (ARPANET)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET)"}, {"title": "birth of the internet a historic development in communications", "url": "https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/anniversary/birth-of-the-internet-a-historic-development-in-communications"}, {"title": "Add Tribune As Your Trusted Source", "url": "https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/anniversary/birth-of-the-internet-a-historic-development-in-communications/amp"}, {"title": "[ih] internet-history Digest, Vol 37, Issue 1", "url": "https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2009-November/000960.html"}, {"title": "30 Years of TCP/IP Dominance Began with a Deadline", "url": "https://internetnews.com/news/30-years-of-tcp-ip-dominance-began-with-a-deadline"}, {"title": "[ih] internet-history Digest, Vol 37, Issue 1", "url": "https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2009-November/000961.html"}, {"title": "Celebrating the Birthday of the Internet", "url": "https://telepolis.de/-3428133"}, {"title": "[ih] Memories of Flag Day?", "url": "https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2023-August/008772.html"}, {"title": "TCP/IP Flag Day Transitionとは?(ティーシーピーアイピーフラッグデー)意味・特徴をわかりやすく解説 | 自作.com | PC自作用語集 - 自作.com", "url": "https://jisaku.com/glossary/tcp-ip-transition-1983"}]}, "The search turned up consistent accounts of the January 1, 1983 cutover, with some useful detail on how strictly it was enforced.\n\n- **The date and basics:** Encyclopedia-style sources place the switch on January 1, 1983, when ARPANET moved its core networking from NCP to the TCP/IP suite ([Wikipedia, Network Control Protocol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET))). Jon Postel's RFC 801 laid out the transition plan in 1981 ([Wikipedia, Flag day](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_day_(computing))).\n- **Scale:** One account puts the host count at about 400, so roughly 400 machines had to change ([InternetNews](https://internetnews.com/news/30-years-of-tcp-ip-dominance-began-with-a-deadline)).\n- **Enforcement:** Backbone routers had already turned off NCP for most hosts on several days in late 1982 as a readiness test ([Wikipedia, NCP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET))). Some sites kept NCP into mid-1983, but only with special permission from backbone operators ([Wikipedia, NCP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Control_Protocol_(ARPANET))). A 2023 recollection from a participant describes an exception list that grew in the first days of 1983 and then shrank as hosts got their TCP/IP stacks working ([ISOC Internet-history list](https://elists.isoc.org/pipermail/internet-history/2023-August/008772.html)).\n- **Aftermath:** In October 1983 ARPANET was split into ARPANET and MILNET, connected through gateways ([Telepolis](https://telepolis.de/-3428133)).\n\nSources are mostly secondary (Wikipedia, news pieces, mailing-list posts). The RFC 801 and Leiner et al. \"Brief History\" citations are the primary references to check if you need firm attribution."], "durationSeconds": 5.913306625000001, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_013TzHm1onkQgNDWUjuV3ZKj", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
